Revision history for Locale-Simple

{{$NEXT}}

 - Perl: l_lang() translates again in a process started under the C locale
   on a system without a locale for the language (a minimal container, a
   daemon without LANG). libintl-perl 1.36 and later ignore LANGUAGE under
   C, so l_lang() now moves LC_MESSAGES to C.UTF-8 in that case.
 - Release tags are now vMAJOR.MINOR.0 (e.g. v0.200.0), valid SemVer for all
   ecosystems. The npm package follows the tag and jumps from 0.1.9 to
   0.200.0 (monotonic, so allowed); npm versions now match the CPAN release.
 - Scraper: recognise PHP sources (.php, option --php for more extensions),
   including heredoc and nowdoc strings and namespace-qualified calls.
   String literal forms now depend on the language type.
 - Scraper: recognise Go sources (.go, option --go): the capitalised helper
   names (L, Ln, ... Ndnp_), with or without package prefix, "..." and
   backtick strings; rune literals no longer open a string.
 - Scraper: recognise Rust sources (.rs, option --rs): the macros l!, ln!, ...
   and the lower-case markers n_!, nn_!, ... with round brackets, "..." and
   raw strings of any hash count, no single-quote strings.
 - JavaScript: evaluate the Plural-Forms header instead of assuming n != 1,
   so Polish, Russian, Arabic, Japanese and other languages pick the right
   plural form. loadTranslations takes the header as optional fourth
   argument; without it the old n != 1 rule stays.
 - PHP: new package raudssus/locale-simple for Composer. The same helpers
   (l, ln, ... Ndnp_) as functions in the namespace Locale\Simple, with its
   own .mo reader and Plural-Forms evaluator, so no ext-gettext is needed.
 - Go: new module github.com/Getty/locale-simple (package in go/) with the
   helpers as L, Ln, ... and the unchanged N_ family, an own .mo reader and
   sprintf, and a Translator value for concurrent, per-request languages.
 - Rust: new crate locale-simple (rust/) with the helpers as macros l!, ln!,
   ... and the lower-case markers n_!, nn_!, ..., an own .mo reader, sprintf
   and Plural-Forms evaluator, and a Translator for concurrent languages.
 - Release: the Rust crate is published to crates.io from the release tag by
   CI (Trusted Publishing), PHP via Packagist and Go via the module proxy
   follow the same tag. dzil test and release also run the PHP, Go and Rust
   suites and skip, visibly, a toolchain that is missing or too old; a CI
   workflow runs all six suites on every push without skipping.
 - .gitattributes keeps the Perl, Python and JavaScript sources out of
   git archive, so a composer require does not pull the whole repository.
 - JavaScript: TypeScript finds the bundled types (src/index.d.ts), and
   the loadLocaleData types accept po2json output with its header.
 - JavaScript: l_nolocales is exported as a no-op, like in the other five
   runtimes.
 - JavaScript: loadLocaleData files a bundle under the language of its
   gettext Language header, so bundles of several languages live side by
   side in one domain.
 - JavaScript: lookups follow the shared locale chain: l_lang('de_AT:fr')
   tries de_AT, de, fr, then a bundle without language. An empty
   translation falls through to the next language, each bundle's own
   plural rule applies, and language tags are normalised (pt-BR is pt_BR).
 - JavaScript: the build uses @rollup/wasm-node and runs on any CPU.
 - Packages: the PyPI wheel and the crates.io crate carry the MIT LICENSE
   file, like the npm package.
 - Scraper: extract calls whose plural count or extra arguments are any
   code (variables, member access, function calls, casts, brackets, strings
   with commas or brackets, calls over several lines) in all six languages,
   together with the translation calls inside those arguments. Before, such
   calls were dropped, e.g. l("Hello %s", $name) or ln(..., items.length).
 - Scraper: files are named by their path relative to the directory the
   scraper runs in, in the #: lines, the file field and the --md5 hash.
 - Scraper: symlinks that point nowhere are skipped and the files after them
   are read.
 - Scraper: string escapes are read the way each language reads them, so the
   .pot holds the msgid the program looks up; "0" is a msgid like any other.
 - Scraper: --js, --pl, --py, --tx, --php, --go and --rs can be given more
   than once, and the lists add up.
 - Scraper: the deferred-translation docs say that N-markers are found in
   .tx templates too.
 - Tests: t/50-scrape.t passes in the CPAN tarball: it leaves out the author
   tests that dzil generates from xt/.
 - Tests: t/10-gettext.t sets the messages locale the way l_lang() does, so
   it passes on systems that start under the C or POSIX locale.
 - Documentation: the READMEs, the POD and the usage skills describe all
   six runtimes, the scraper's languages and loading po2json output in
   JavaScript.

0.109     2026-04-23 13:40:35Z

 - Deferred-translation markers (N_, Nn_, Np_, Nd_, Ndn_, Ndp_, Nnp_,
   Ndnp_) — mirror the l-family for class-construction-time use.
 - Scraper: recognise the eight N*_ names (Perl / JS / Python) and emit
   .pot entries identical to their l*() twins. .tx templates are not
   scanned for N-markers on purpose.
 - Scraper: Ldp now recognised by the parser (was listed in the arg-map
   but missing from the function regex / required-args table).

0.108     2026-04-22 04:35:31Z

 - Modernize build: [@Author::GETTY] bundle, cpanfile, drop weaver.ini/setup.py
 - Python: fix save.push->append, make bind_textdomain_codeset optional, drop
   deprecated lgettext, move to pyproject.toml
 - JavaScript: rewrite as modern ESM/CJS npm package with own sprintf, drop
   dead Gettext.js dependency
 - POD cleanup in Locale::Simple (SUPPORT section via bundle)

0.019     2016-12-15

 - Previous CPAN release (see git history for pre-modernization changes)
